{"task":"transcribe","language":"en","duration":136.384,"text":"I'd appreciate if you could lay out the plan in different steps I can execute on. The way I want to handle this evaluation is I have my training script that has early stopping implemented. I then so when that's complete, whatever the best checkpoint is gets logged in weights and biases. I then want to run the evile.py script, which is the pasted text for attachment, to get the metrics I can use for comparison. And so ideally what I'd like is a script or like a way to write scripts where I can just run through a number of different hyperparameters, evaluate them, and then just pull all that data, compare them, pick something, and then move on to the next step. In terms of like in terms of how I would perform this search, I am debating whether or not I should just do one big tuning run that will include everything from learning rate and batch size to class and balance and augmentations. Just throw them all at once and then see what combination ends up working out the best. The only caveat I have here is that augmentations will probably be harder to script. And in my testing, I've had mixed, very mixed results with using augmentations. Whenever I've used augmentations, I always end up with bad or like weird performance behaviors. And so because I'm a little crunched for time, I'm skeptical as to like fully pursuing the augmentation route, especially because in some of the preliminary tests I've done, the unaugmented runs, which may use weighted loss or oversampling, do perform decently well. What do you think and how do you think I should approach this?","segments":[{"id":0,"start":0.0,"end":7.2,"text":"I'd appreciate if you could lay out the plan in different steps I can execute on."},{"id":1,"start":7.5200000000000005,"end":17.92,"text":"The way I want to handle this evaluation is I have my training script that has early stopping implemented."},{"id":2,"start":19.44,"end":25.76,"text":"I then so when that's complete, whatever the best checkpoint is gets logged in weights and biases."},{"id":3,"start":26.080000000000002,"end":36.32,"text":"I then want to run the evile.py script, which is the pasted text for attachment, to get the metrics I can use for comparison."},{"id":4,"start":36.64,"end":54.56,"text":"And so ideally what I'd like is a script or like a way to write scripts where I can just run through a number of different hyperparameters, evaluate them, and then just pull all that data, compare them, pick something, and then move on to the next step."},{"id":5,"start":55.84,"end":87.12,"text":"In terms of like in terms of how I would perform this search, I am debating whether or not I should just do one big tuning run that will include everything from learning rate and batch size to class and balance and augmentations."},{"id":6,"start":87.76,"end":92.32000000000001,"text":"Just throw them all at once and then see what combination ends up working out the best."},{"id":7,"start":92.64,"end":99.2,"text":"The only caveat I have here is that augmentations will probably be harder to script."},{"id":8,"start":99.52,"end":105.2,"text":"And in my testing, I've had mixed, very mixed results with using augmentations."},{"id":9,"start":105.52,"end":114.4,"text":"Whenever I've used augmentations, I always end up with bad or like weird performance behaviors."},{"id":10,"start":114.64,"end":132.8,"text":"And so because I'm a little crunched for time, I'm skeptical as to like fully pursuing the augmentation route, especially because in some of the preliminary tests I've done, the unaugmented runs, which may use weighted loss or oversampling, do perform decently well."},{"id":11,"start":133.12,"end":136.08,"text":"What do you think and how do you think I should approach this?"}]}